\section{Almost-Global Proxy Entry to the Live Capture Region}
\label{sec:semiglobal-stability}

The preceding results answer different stability questions.  Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-block-local}
establishes analytically that the normal full-heading Live recursion has a local
block-ISS neighborhood relative to its adopted heading gauge.  Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture}
is the operational full-heading result: it certifies a larger structured
post-handoff region from which the complete nonlinear OU--III recursion reaches
a robustly invariant regional set in finite time and then satisfies a direct
practical-ISS bound.  Startup therefore has only to place the initialized
filter inside the outer regional capture set.  Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid-live-recovery}
then stitches the normal-Live intervals across the one-time magnetic refinement
and guarded tilt re-lock jumps.  No-magnetometer operation is a different
symmetry class and is covered locally by the quotient result of
Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-nomag-quotient}; it does not inherit an absolute-heading
capture claim.

``Semiglobal'' below means uniformity on any prescribed compact startup set
contained in the desired proxy domain of attraction.  It does not claim
semiglobal convergence of an already-running MEKF from arbitrary 21-state
errors, and it does not turn the local MEKF chart into a global coordinate
system.

\subsection{Compatibility with the deployed physical-MSE schedule}
\label{sec:semiglobal-mse-schedule}

Neither the regional capture theorem nor the local ISS theorem requires a
particular monomial adaptation law.  They require the predictable compact
schedule of Eqs.~\eqref{eq:iss-step-tau-bounds}--
\eqref{eq:iss-tuning-bounds}.  For the deployed SpectralMSE regularizer, the
physical-MSE calculation gives
\begin{equation}
 r_{S,\star}
 =C_J q_{\rm eff}^{1/14}\,
 \widehat\sigma_{a,B}^{\,6/7}\,
 \tau_\star^{24/7}T_S^{-1/2},
 \label{eq:semiglobal-mse-schedule}
\end{equation}
and, away from the cadence clamps where $T_S=c_T\tau$,
\begin{equation}
 r_{S,\star}\propto
 q_{\rm eff}^{1/14}\widehat\sigma_{a,B}^{\,6/7}
 \tau^{41/14}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-mse-powers}
\end{equation}
No $\sigma\tau^3$ adaptation relation is used in this proof.  Finite bounds on
$q_{\rm eff}$, $\widehat\sigma_{a,B}$, $T_S$, and $\tau$, together with the
implementation clamps and convex smoothing, keep the applied
$(\tau,\sigma_{aw},r_S)$ schedule in a compact predictable family.  That family
is included directly in $\Pi_{k,m}$ in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-comparison-envelope}; the proof follows the actual staged
schedule rather than replacing it by one common worst-case contraction factor.

\subsection{Reduced Mahony proxy dynamics}
\label{sec:semiglobal-proxy}

Let $\vct d\in\mathbb S^2$ denote the true body-frame direction of world down
and $\widehat{\vct d}_P\in\mathbb S^2$ the direction predicted by the startup
proxy.  Only tilt is observable from this channel, so define
\begin{equation}
 \theta_P=\cos^{-1}(\widehat{\vct d}_P^\top\vct d),
 \qquad 0\le\theta_P\le\pi,
 \label{eq:semiglobal-tilt-error}
\end{equation}
and let $\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}$ be the component of proxy gyro-bias
error perpendicular to $\vct d$.  Rotation about $\vct d$ and the corresponding
parallel bias component are gauge variables for the gravity-only proxy.  The
reduced state is therefore
\begin{equation}
 \vct z_P=(\widehat{\vct d}_P,\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}),
\end{equation}
with desired set
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal A_P=\{\widehat{\vct d}_P=\vct d,
                 \widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}=0\}.
\end{equation}
The smooth single-vector correction also has the undesired antipodal critical
set $\mathcal U_P$.  With integral bias estimation, the excluded set is the
stable set of those undesired equilibria,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathcal N_P:=W^s(\mathcal U_P),\qquad
 \mathcal D_P:=
 (\mathbb S^2\times\mathbb R^2)\setminus\mathcal N_P .}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-domain}
\end{equation}
For the disturbance-free reduced dynamics induced by
Eq.~\eqref{eq:proxy-mahony}, the Mahony construction gives almost-global
asymptotic convergence to $\mathcal A_P$; the complement of
$\mathcal D_P$ has measure zero \cite{Mahony2008NonlinearComplementary}.

Fix any compact
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal K_{\epsilon,B_\beta}\subset\mathcal D_P,
 \qquad
 \operatorname{dist}(\mathcal K_{\epsilon,B_\beta},\mathcal N_P)
 \ge\epsilon>0,
 \qquad
 \|\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}\|\le B_\beta.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-compact}
\end{equation}
A converse Lyapunov theorem on a compact forward neighborhood gives a smooth
$V_\epsilon$ and class-$\mathcal K$ functions with
\begin{equation}
 \alpha_{1,\epsilon}(|\vct z_P|_{\mathcal A_P})
 \le V_\epsilon
 \le\alpha_{2,\epsilon}(|\vct z_P|_{\mathcal A_P}),
 \qquad
 \dot V_\epsilon
 \le-\alpha_{3,\epsilon}(|\vct z_P|_{\mathcal A_P})
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-converse}
\end{equation}
\cite{Khalil2002_NonlinearSystems}.  Let $\vct u_P$ collect bounded wave-induced
specific-force perturbation in the normalized direction, gyro noise, slow
bias/model mismatch, and the finite-step defect.  Assume
\begin{equation}
 0<f_{P,0}\le\|\vct f_b(t)\|\le f_{P,1}<\infty
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-force-bound}
\end{equation}
so accelerometer normalization remains smooth on the prescribed compact set.
Smoothness gives a finite $L_\epsilon$ and standard comparison yields
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 |\vct z_P(t)|_{\mathcal A_P}
 \le
 \beta_\epsilon(|\vct z_P(0)|_{\mathcal A_P},t)
 +\gamma_\epsilon(\|\vct u_P\|_\infty).}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-iss}
\end{equation}
Thus the proxy is semiglobally practically ISS on every compact subset of
$\mathcal D_P$, with constants that need not remain uniform as the initial set
approaches $\mathcal N_P$.

\subsection{Quality gate, tilt error, and heading gauge}
\label{sec:semiglobal-handoff}

The proxy is not required to converge to the final Live invariant region.  It
need only make the subsequent MEKF handoff belong to the structured outer set
of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture}.  The implemented gravity gate uses
\begin{equation}
 \widehat{\vct s}_P=-\widehat{\vct d}_P,
 \qquad
 \widehat{\vct s}_{\rm meas}
 =\frac{\vct f_{\rm LP}}{\|\vct f_{\rm LP}\|},
\end{equation}
and requires
\begin{equation}
 \|\widehat{\vct s}_P\times\widehat{\vct s}_{\rm meas}\|
 \le0.075.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-sine-gate}
\end{equation}
Because a sine residual is identical at $\alpha$ and $\pi-\alpha$, every
accepted handoff also tests
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \widehat{\vct s}_P^\top\widehat{\vct s}_{\rm meas}>0,}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-aligned-branch}
\end{equation}
selecting the aligned branch.  If $\eta_g$ bounds the angular difference between
the low-pass measured direction and true rest specific force during the
accepted hold, then
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\delta\vct\theta_{\perp,H}\|
 \le\overline\theta_{\perp,H}
 :=\sin^{-1}(0.075)+\eta_g.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-tilt}
\end{equation}

For a full-heading handoff, let the accepted magnetic or equivalent reference
define the adopted heading gauge of Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-heading-gauge}.  Let
$\eta_m$ bound the \emph{internal} heading error relative to that adopted gauge,
\begin{equation}
 |\delta\psi_{\rm int,H}|\le\eta_m.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-yaw-internal}
\end{equation}
The residual reference bias $b_{\psi,\rm ref}$ is not part of the MEKF capture
error.  Relative to true north,
\begin{equation}
 |\delta\psi_{\rm true,H}|
 \le\eta_m+|b_{\psi,\rm ref}|,
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-yaw-true}
\end{equation}
but only the first term belongs in the stability radius.  A conservative local
three-axis gauge-referenced attitude bound is therefore
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\delta\vct\theta_H^{(g)}\|
 \le\overline\theta_H^{(g)}
 :=\overline\theta_{\perp,H}+\eta_m<\pi.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-attitude}
\end{equation}
This prevents an irreducible compass/reference offset from being mistaken for a
failure of estimator convergence.

The proxy does not estimate every MEKF state and therefore must not be credited
with shrinking them.  Let the physical startup family provide finite bounds
\begin{align}
 \|\delta\vct b_{g,H}\|&\le B_{g,H},&
 \|\delta\vct S_H\|&\le B_{S,H},\\
 \|\delta\vct a_{w,H}\|&\le B_{aw,H},&
 \|\delta\vct b_{a,H}\|&\le B_{a,H},
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-sensitive-bounds}
\end{align}
and
\begin{equation}
 \|\delta\vct v_H\|\le B_{v,H},
 \qquad
 \|\delta\vct p_H\|\le B_{p,H}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-kinematic-bounds}
\end{equation}
For compatibility with the block-local notation define
\begin{align}
 B_{\xi,H}
 &:={\left[
 (\overline\theta_H^{(g)}/s_\theta)^2
 +(B_{g,H}/s_{bg})^2
 +(B_{S,H}/s_S)^2
 +(B_{aw,H}/s_{aw})^2
 +(B_{a,H}/s_{ba})^2\right]^{1/2}},\\
 B_{\ell,H}
 &:={\left[
 (B_{v,H}/s_v)^2+(B_{p,H}/s_p)^2\right]^{1/2}}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-block-bounds}
\end{align}
These are physical handoff bounds relative to the adopted heading gauge, not a
claim that the handoff already lies in the local neighborhood of
Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-block-local}.

Let $\Pi_H$ denote the compact set of staged handoff tuning/covariance modes and
let $\mathcal H$ be the structured gauge-referenced physical-error set defined
above.  In the regional metrics of Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-structured-radii},
define
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \vct R_H^{\max}:=
 \begin{bmatrix}
 \displaystyle
 \sup_{\pi\in\Pi_H}\sup_{\vct e\in\mathcal H}r_{\xi,H}(\vct e)\\[1ex]
 \displaystyle
 \sup_{\pi\in\Pi_H}\sup_{\vct e\in\mathcal H}r_{\ell,H}(\vct e)
 \end{bmatrix}.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-metric-radius}
\end{equation}
The proxy-to-Live interface condition is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \vct R_H^{\max}\prec\vct R_C.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-capture-entry}
\end{equation}
No prescribed outer/inner radius ratio is required.

\begin{theorem}[Almost-global/semiglobal proxy-to-Live finite capture]
\label{thm:semiglobal-proxy-live}
Fix $\mathcal K_{\epsilon,B_\beta}$ as in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-compact} and a compact physical startup family
satisfying Eqs.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-force-bound},
\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-sensitive-bounds}, and
\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-kinematic-bounds}.  Assume:
(i) the proxy initial error belongs to $\mathcal K_{\epsilon,B_\beta}$;
(ii) its bounded perturbation is small enough for
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-iss} to reach a quality-gated gravity interval;
(iii) the accepted interval satisfies
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-aligned-branch};
(iv) tuner readiness and an adopted magnetic/equivalent heading gauge become
available in finite time uniformly over the startup family, and subsequent
full-heading Live windows satisfy either route (a) or (b) of
Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-21-ues};
(v) the resulting gauge-referenced handoff family satisfies
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-capture-entry}; and
(vi) the exact-map regional certificate of
Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture}, including its predictable schedule,
prefix-domain, and no-hard-reset assumptions, holds through the capture
interval.  Initialize the certified comparison sequence with
$\overline{\vct r}^{\,H}_0=\vct R_H^{\max}$ and let $N_H$ be
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-count}.  Then there is a finite uniform handoff index
$k_H$ and a finite capture index $k_C$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 k_C-k_H\le\sum_{n=0}^{N_H-1}m_n,
 \qquad
 t_C-t_H\le h_{\max}\sum_{n=0}^{N_H-1}m_n.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-capture-count}
\end{equation}
At $k_C$ the gauge-referenced Live error belongs to $\mathcal B_{k_C}$, and
subsequent post-capture lifted errors satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\vct r_{k_{C+j}}\|_{\vct w,\infty}
 \le
 \lambda_B^j\|\vct r_{k_C}\|_{\vct w,\infty}
 +\frac{1-\lambda_B^j}{1-\lambda_B}\gamma_B(\overline d).}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-live-bound}
\end{equation}
Thus zero bounded deterministic Live disturbance gives exponential convergence
to the adopted heading gauge after finite capture, while a bounded deterministic
mismatch gives the regional practical-ISS neighborhood.  A constant residual
heading-reference bias remains a gauge offset rather than a nonzero MEKF error.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equation~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-iss} gives uniform practical convergence on
the prescribed compact proxy set.  Under assumptions (ii)--(iv), compactness
gives a finite uniform time at which the gravity, branch, tuner, and adopted
heading-gauge conditions for normal full-heading handoff are satisfied.
Equations~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-attitude}--
\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-kinematic-bounds} place the initialized
\emph{gauge-referenced} MEKF error in $\mathcal H$.  The residual true-north
offset $b_{\psi,\rm ref}$ is excluded by construction.  Definition
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-metric-radius} bounds this handoff set by
$\vct R_H^{\max}$, and assumption (v) places it inside the outer regional
capture set.

Apply the monotone comparison-envelope argument of
Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture} beginning from
$\vct R_H^{\max}$.  Assumption (vi) keeps every prefix inside the proof domain,
permits certified early expansion, and by the definition of $N_H$ places the
error in $\mathcal B_{k_C}$ after the finite sample/time bound
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-capture-count}.  Robust invariance and post-capture
contraction give Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-live-bound}.  Finally,
$\mathcal N_P$ has measure zero, so the attitude-startup qualification is
almost global while the complete proxy-to-Live statement is semiglobal on each
prescribed compact physical startup family.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{No-magnetometer branch.}
Before an absolute heading gauge exists, or on hardware operated permanently
without one, the gravity-only proxy and Live model possess a yaw symmetry.
Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-nomag-quotient} gives local UES only modulo that symmetry.
The full-heading regional capture theorem above is therefore not silently
reused for no-mag mode; a future regional certificate for that mode must use a
gauge-invariant quotient metric.

\paragraph{Relation to the block-local theorem.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-block-local} remains an independent analytical statement
about the local normal-Live equilibrium.  It is not an additional set-inclusion
test in the operational startup chain.

\paragraph{Finite startup timeout.}
Almost-global convergence is not uniform as the undesired stable set is
approached, so a fixed \SI{150}{s} policy cannot certify all compact startup
sets simultaneously.  The implementation withholds a timeout-forced handoff
until Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-aligned-branch} holds.  The timeout path does not
require the 0.075 residual and therefore does not inherit
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-tilt}.  It is covered by the composite theorem
only when an independent timeout attitude bound defines a handoff set
$\mathcal H_T$ whose structured envelope
$\vct R_{H,T}^{\max}\prec\vct R_C$.

\paragraph{Later reconfiguration.}
The one-time magnetic refinement and Live tilt re-lock are now part of the
hybrid stability architecture rather than unmodelled breaks in it.  Under the
jump-family conditions of Eqs.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-mag-heading-residual},
\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-force-bound}, and
\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-yaw-chart}, and the jump-entry test
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-jump-entry}, Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid-live-recovery}
returns either event to the outer capture set and proves safety across repeated
reconfiguration.  If hard resets cease, finite capture and practical ISS resume
after the final jump.  Persistent re-locks give recurrent capture only when the
inter-jump intervals are long enough for the verified recovery sequence.

\paragraph{Stochastic qualification.}
The deterministic Mahony-to-capture result uses bounded perturbations for the
startup proxy and bounded deterministic mismatch during finite capture.
Gaussian Live process and measurement noise are treated by
Theorem~\ref{thm:capture-stochastic-live} under its separate drift hypothesis.